parts.text:
Now appstore.cpp. Changes:

1. Constants: define a draft-mode string constant? Many `m_mode == QStringLiteral("draft")` checks. Add `isDraftMode()` and `isRecordMode()` helpers for "record"/"record-edit". Let me add namespace-level constants:

```cpp
const char *ModeDraft = "draft";
const char *ModeRecord = "record";
const char *ModeRecordEdit = "record-edit";
```

Actually many of the existing checks use QStringLiteral("draft"). I'll add helper functions in the class:

```cpp
bool AppStore::isDraftMode() const { return m_mode == QStringLiteral("draft"); }
bool AppStore::isRecordMode() const { return m_mode == QStringLiteral("record") || m_mode == QStringLiteral("record-edit"); }
```

I declared isRecordMode in header; also isDraftMode. Add to header too. Let me add both. Actually I already added isRecordMode. Add isDraftMode as well.

2. rectanglesChanged lambda connects canCommitChanged — should also emit canSaveEditChanged when in edit mode; simpler: also connect dataChanged/rows etc to emit canSaveEditChanged. Add `emit canSaveEditChanged();` in lambda... but emitting unnecessary is fine.

Actually canSaveEdit depends on editTag/editDate only (not rectangles) — rectangle count doesn't matter for saving an edit? For commit we required at least 1 rectangle. For saving an edit, should we require at least one rectangle too? Reasonable to keep records non-empty: require at least one rectangle as well. canSaveEdit = m_editRectangle? Let me define: mode == record-edit && editDate valid && editTag non-empty. I won't require rectangles (record could remain empty? draft commit requires one.) For consistency require ≥1 rectangle: rectangles model count > 0 when in edit mode. The changed lambda already emits canCommitChanged; add canSaveEditChanged too.

3. Property getters: editDate, editTag, editNotes, editColor, editingRecord (`m_mode == record-edit`), canSaveEdit.

4. Setters setEditDate etc. — in-memory only, emit changes + draftContent? No, emit canSaveEditChanged. editColor — also emit currentRectangleColorChanged.

5. currentRectangleColor: return editColor in record-edit mode.

6. selectRecord: block if mode == "record-edit" with error "Save or cancel your edits first." Also there's the guard state — only if record-edit; resumeDraft: if mode == record-edit, block similarly ("Save or cancel your record edits first."). Actually resumeDraft from "record" mode is fine. Also clearDraft and addDraftRectangle etc. are guarded by draft mode so no issue. commitDraft: mode check remains draft.

7. startRecordEdit():
```cpp
void AppStore::startRecordEdit()
{
    if (!m_hasMap) return;
    if (m_mode != QStringLiteral("record")) { ...error... }
    // m_viewedRecordId should be valid
    QString error;
    const auto record = m_database->record(m_viewedRecordId, &error);
    if (!record) {...}
    m_editDate = record->date; m_editTag=...; m_editNotes=...; m_editColor=...;
    loadRecordRectangles(m_viewedRecordId); // loads into m_rectangles
    // emit edit* changed + canSaveEditChanged
    setMode("record-edit");
    setStatus("Editing the saved application.");
}
```
But loadRecordRectangles emits canCommitChanged, fine.

8. cancelRecordEdit():
```cpp
if (!editingRecord()) return;
const int recordId = m_viewedRecordId;
// reload record to view (ensures clean state)
const auto record = ...;
m_viewedRecord = recordToMap(*record);
loadRecordRectangles(recordId);
mode -> "record";
```
Actually mode change from record-edit to record. Keep viewedRecordId; emit viewedRecordChanged, edit*? Clear edit state (not strictly necessary). Set status "Cancelled edits...". Hmm, just reload from db and set mode "record".

9. saveRecordEdit():
```cpp
if (m_mode != record-edit) return;
if (!canSaveEdit()) { setError("Enter a tag and a valid date before saving."); return; }
const int recordId = m_viewedRecordId;
Record fields{date, tag trimmed, color, notes};
QVector<Rect> stored from m_rectangles;
if (!m_database->updateRecord(...)) { setError(error); return; }
refreshRecords();
const auto record = m_database->record(recordId, &error);
m_viewedRecord = recordToMap; emit viewedRecordChanged();
loadRecordRectangles(recordId);
setMode("record"); status "Record updated."
```

10. Rectangle ops guards: addDraftRectangle, updateDraftRectangle, removeDraftRectangle, saveDraftRectangles:

Current guards: `if (m_mode != QStringLiteral("draft")) return;`
Replace with `if (!isDraftMode() && !editingRecord()) return;`

persistDraftRectangles must only run in draft mode → guard inside persistDraftRectangles: `if (m_mode not draft) return;` Actually persistDraftRectangles is called from selectRecord (mode draft at call time) — selectRecord persists draft then switches to record. So the calls are made while still draft. If I guard inside persistDraftRectangles with mode==draft it's fine: at call time mode is draft. But cleaner: in the rectangle ops, only call persistDraftRectangles() when isDraftMode(). saveDraftRectangles: only update m_draftRectangleCount and persist when draft mode; in record-edit mode just return (keep memory-only). Wait but map release calls App.saveDraftRectangles() in both modes. In record-edit mode it should not touch draft. So:

```cpp
void AppStore::saveDraftRectangles()
{
    if (editingRecord())
        return; // edits stay in memory until saveRecordEdit()
    if (!isDraftMode())
        return;
    ...
}
```

removeDraftRectangle adjusts m_draftRectangleCount — only meaningful in draft; guard: only set m_draftRectangleCount when draft mode (or keep as is minus persist?). m_draftRectangleCount is used in draftHasContent and canCommit. When in record-edit mode, m_rectangles holds the record's rectangles, so m_draftRectangleCount shouldn't change; but draftHasContent computed with rectangleCount? draftHasContent uses m_draftRectangleCount. So in record-edit mode, don't touch m_draftRectangleCount. In removeDraftRectangle:

```cpp
m_rectangles.removeRectangle(index);
if (isDraftMode()) { m_draftRectangleCount = m_rectangles.count(); persist...; }
emit draftContentChanged(); // harmless
```

Hmm but emitting draftContentChanged in record-edit — harmless.

addDraftRectangle: `++m_draftRectangleCount` only in draft mode.

updateDraftRectangle: mode guard only.

11. commitDraft error message: "Resume editing before committing a new application." → "Edit the new/uncommitted application before committing."

12. resumeDraft status message: "Editing the new/uncommitted application."

13. persistEditFields — not needed since nothing persists until save. I declared persistEditFields in header; drop it (remove). I'll remove from header.

Also rectanglesChanged lambda: add `canSaveEditChanged`.

Also setMode: emit canSaveEditChanged on mode change.

Also loadDraft resets edit state? On loadDraft (resume draft), edit fields not relevant; but mode set to draft so editingRecord false, save button hidden. Fine.

Also selectRecord while editing blocked. Where is selectRecord called — RecordList onClicked. Blocking is fine.

Edge: viewing a record without editing, then pressing "Edit record" — startRecordEdit requires mode==record... but what if mode=="record" yet no viewed record? m_viewedRecordId >= 0 since we're in record mode. Guard anyway.

Let me now make the edits in appstore.h (remove persistEditFields, add isDraftMode isn't needed—can use editingRecord() and inline checks). I'll keep isRecordMode for record+record-edit checks? Not needed anywhere... cancelRecordEdit loads record... skip. Remove isRecordMode from header since unused.

